 as a worker",[252,972,411],{},[252,974],{},[252,976],{},[252,978],{},[252,980],{},[252,982],{},[252,984],{},[252,986],{},[221,988,989,995,997,999,1001,1003,1005,1007,1009],{},[252,990,991,992,903],{},"Tool allowlist sandbox (",[900,993,994],{},"allowedTools",[252,996,411],{},[252,998],{},[252,1000],{},[252,1002],{},[252,1004],{},[252,1006],{},[252,1008],{},[252,1010],{},[221,1012,1013,1016,1018,1020,1022,1024,1026,1028,1030],{},[252,1014,1015],{},"Alerts on a missed slot \u002F final run",[252,1017,411],{},[252,1019],{},[252,1021],{},[252,1023],{},[252,1025],{},[252,1027],{},[252,1029],{},[252,1031],{},[150,1033,1034,1035,1038,1039,1042,1043,1046,1047,1050,1051,1054,1055,157,1058,357,1061,1064,1065,1068,1069,1072,1073,1076],{},"¹ ",[154,1036,1037],{},"Delayed jobs, sched's flavour."," A delay is expressed as a ",[154,1040,1041],{},"one-shot schedule",", not as\na property of an enqueued job: ",[900,1044,1045],{},"{ \"once\": \"tomorrow at noon\" }"," in ",[900,1048,1049],{},"tasks.json",", or\n",[900,1052,1053],{},"POST \u002Fschedules"," at runtime — a real schedule entity carrying ",[900,1056,1057],{},"data",[900,1059,1060],{},"tz",[900,1062,1063],{},"dedupKey",",\nwhich fires once and then unschedules. What sched does ",[202,1066,1067],{},"not"," have is BullMQ's per-job\n",[900,1070,1071],{},"delay(ms)"," on an immediate trigger: ",[900,1074,1075],{},"POST \u002Ftasks\u002F:name\u002Frun"," starts the run now.",[150,1078,1079,1080,1083,1084,1087,1088,1093,1094,1097],{},"² ",[154,1081,1082],{},"\"Sandboxed worker\" means something else here, so sched's cell is empty."," In BullMQ\nand Bull it is process isolation — the job runs in a separate child process. sched has no\nsuch isolation; its sandbox is a ",[202,1085,1086],{},"policy",": each run is checked against\n",[179,1089,1091],{"href":1090},"security",[900,1092,994],{}," (an allowlist of commands, images or MCP ",[900,1095,1096],{},"server:tool"," specs)\nbefore it is allowed to spawn. That is why the allowlist appears as its own row.",[210,1099,1101],{"id":1100},"what-the-table-does-not-say","What the table does not say",[1103,1104,1105,1112,1124,1130],"ul",{},[1106,1107,1108,1111],"li",{},[154,1109,1110],{},"Schedulers versus queues."," sched and Agenda are cron schedulers with a job queue\nattached. BullMQ, Bull and Bee are queues that also happen to support repeatable jobs.\nIf your unit of work is \"a message that must be consumed fast and exactly once\", that is\ntheir home ground, not ours.",[1106,1113,1114,1117,1118,1123],{},[154,1115,1116],{},"The paid line."," BullMQ-Pro's ticks are commercial features of that package. The UI\nrows are also not always in the box: Agenda's UI is the separate\n",[179,1119,1122],{"href":1120,"rel":1121},"https:\u002F\u002Fgithub.com\u002Fagenda\u002Fagenda\u002Ftree\u002Fmain\u002Fpackages\u002Fagendash",[183],"Agendash"," package, and\nBullMQ's open-source dashboard is the separate Bull Board project.",[1106,1125,1126,1129],{},[154,1127,1128],{},"Blanks are blanks."," An empty cell above means the source chart did not claim the\nfeature and we did not test it — not that the feature is missing.",[1106,1131,1132,1135],{},[154,1133,1134],{},"No benchmarks."," Nothing on this page is a throughput or latency claim. Compare those\non your own workload.",[210,1137,1139],{"id":1138},"when-to-pick-something-else","When to pick something else",[1103,1141,1142,1150,1158,1165,1171],{},[1106,1143,1144,1147,1148,174],{},[154,1145,1146],{},"High-throughput queue with rate limiting, retries and a worker fleet in Redis"," →\n",[154,1149,156],{},[1106,1151,1152,1155,1156,174],{},[154,1153,1154],{},"Already on Postgres and you want queues in the same database"," → ",[154,1157,169],{},[1106,1159,1160,1155,1163,174],{},[154,1161,1162],{},"On MongoDB, want minimal overhead, and the chart above fits you",[154,1164,173],{},[1106,1166,1167,1170],{},[154,1168,1169],{},"On Bull, Kue or Bee"," → they have not shipped since 2024\u002F2025; plan a migration to\nwhichever of the living options fits.",[1106,1172,1173,1176],{},[154,1174,1175],{},"You need durable multi-step workflows"," (steps, long sleeps, signals) → that is a\ndifferent class of tool entirely.",[210,1178,1180],{"id":1179},"sources","Sources",[1103,1182,1183,1189,1195,1202],{},[1106,1184,1185,1186],{},"Agenda's feature comparison chart — ",[179,1187,181],{"href":181,"rel":1188},[183],[1106,1190,1191,1192],{},"BullMQ's feature comparison chart — ",[179,1193,193],{"href":193,"rel":1194},[183],[1106,1196,1197,1198],{},"pg-boss README (cron\u002FRRULE, maintenance model) — ",[179,1199,1200],{"href":1200,"rel":1201},"https:\u002F\u002Fgithub.com\u002Ftimgit\u002Fpg-boss",[183],[1106,1203,1204,1205,157,1208,157,1211,1214,1215,157,1218,157,1220,1223],{},"Release dates and licenses — npm registry metadata for ",[900,1206,1207],{},"bullmq",[900,1209,1210],{},"bull",[900,1212,1213],{},"kue",",\n",[900,1216,1217],{},"bee-queue",[900,1219,169],{},[900,1221,1222],{},"agenda",", checked 2026-09-19.",{"title":1225,"searchDepth":21,"depth":21,"links":1226},"",[1227,1228,1229,1230,1231],{"id":212,"depth":21,"text":213},{"id":371,"depth":21,"text":372},{"id":1100,"depth":21,"text":1101},{"id":1138,"depth":21,"text":1139},{"id":1179,"depth":21,"text":1180},"sched next to the Node.js job schedulers and queues it is usually weighed against — BullMQ, Bull, Kue, Bee, pg-boss and Agenda","md",null,{},true,{"title":14,"description":1232},"fJ7omrQLsww2KdmPjraEtAv_iiB42flFePqrWD8fOWY",[1240,1242],{"title":10,"path":11,"stem":12,"description":1241,"children":-1},"What sched is and how it works",{"title":18,"path":19,"stem":20,"description":1243,"order":21,"children":-1},"Three ways to run sched — pick your scale",1789905804032]